Z||000008||kslish||07-28-2001||03:58 AM||||Well, since I can't sleep, I guess I'll answer this one....<P>The dogbone that is in the first picture is the newer-style stock replacement dogbone (also factory stock on '87-'88 Fieros although I can't remember when GM made the switch, maybe even as early as sometime in '86).  They moved the reinforcing rib to the inside of the bone and started using cast-in rubber bushings.  These dogbones cannot be rebushed due to the conical shaped insides and once the bushings are shot, the bone is best thrown into the trash bin.  This is the style of dogbone that you'll find available at your local auto parts store or the GM dealer.<P>The second picture that Bluebottlejunky posted shows an original-style dogbone that an '85 model year GT was equipped with from the factory.  These have the reinforcing rib around the outside of the bone and the cylindrical rubber bushings are pressed in.  This style of dogbone is no longer available new, but they do have the advantage of being able to be rebushed with polyurethane when the rubber wears out (just press out the old rubber and put in the new poly bushings).  <P>Hope this clear the confusion up.....<P><P>------------------<BR>Ken S.<BR>'87GT and '86SC<BR>See them at <A HREF="http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero.html" TARGET=_blank>http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero.html</A><BR>Western PA Fiero Club - <A HREF="http://www.westpennfiero.org" TARGET=_blank>www.westpennfiero.org</A>||||reg||1
